# An instance head carrying a CONCRETE type argument. This is the shape that
# made the head-granular `@inst:` key unsound, and it is why the Haskell 2010
# §4.3.2 restriction exists.
#
# Before this rule, the program below COMPILED AND RAN. `register_instance_marker`
# derives its key through `type_head_str`, whose `TApp base _` arm discards every
# argument — so `List Int` and `List String` are the same key, `@inst:Describe:List`.
# Nothing downstream ever compared the instance's argument to the call site's, so
# `describe(strs())` selected the `List Int` body and its `Int` arithmetic ran on
# String payloads: `--phase check` reported OK, clang linked cleanly, and the
# program printed a sum of two String heap pointers (8737893664 on one run).
#
# Note there is only ONE instance here — `check_overlapping_instances` cannot fire.
# The defect is not a collision between instances; it is that a single instance
# answers for types it was never written for.
class Describe a {
  fn describe(x: a) -> String
}

fn add(acc: Int, n: Int) -> Int = acc + n

instance Describe (List Int) {
  fn describe(x: List Int) -> String = int_to_string(list_fold(add, 0, x))
}

fn strs() -> List String = Cons("hello", Cons("world", Nil))

fn main() -> Unit !{IO} = print(describe(strs()))
